    summary_en: "A new type of autonomous malware, ClosedQuorum, has emerged, capable of lurking within internal networks. This malware leverages the computational power of commercial Large Language Models (LLMs) to bypass existing security defenses and penetrate deep into internal infrastructure in real-time. Unlike traditional malware that uses fixed scripts, ClosedQuorum receives real-time AI inference instructions, allowing it to effectively evade existing detection mechanisms. Experts point out that to prevent this 'thinking' malware from autonomously infiltrating, strict controls must be implemented at the endpoint level, specifically by blocking unauthorized AI API communications and establishing robust outbound traffic control mechanisms. This indicates that enterprises must upgrade their security architecture, shifting from simple signature detection to deep monitoring at the behavioral and traffic layers.",
